# The first three lines are the ones that matter. A `COPY . .` in a Dockerfile
# is how credentials end up baked into an image layer, where they survive
# `docker rmi` of the tag, get pushed to a registry, and stay readable in the
# layer history long after the file was "deleted". The Dockerfile here copies
# named paths rather than `.`, so this is a second independent guard — but the
# whole point of a second guard is that it holds when the first one changes.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*

.git/
.gitignore
.github/

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# Outputs, not inputs. Traces from a previous run in the build context would be
# copied into the image and then shadowed by the volume mount — confusing, and
# they can be large.
traces/
*.jsonl

# Test and benchmark scaffolding is not needed at runtime and only enlarges the
# image and its attack surface.
tests/
benchmarks/
docs/
*.md
!README.md
